{"w": 595.3200073242188, "h": 809.0257568359375, "lines": [{"t": "48", "x": 0.9030101634011957, "y": 0.9742087522985531, "w": 0.030733085202440134, "h": 0.017428414981790374, "c": 1, "size": 12.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "have shown that molluscs and fish make up a small part of their diets. West Indian", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.12319506908974809, "w": 0.8263163973387393, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "and Amazonian manatees are both herbivores.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.15152541916388573, "w": 0.473014996427518, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "Manatees' teeth are all molars - flat, rounded teeth for grinding food. Due to", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.17970752416545568, "w": 0.8266092086068059, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "manatees' abrasive aquatic plant diet, these teeth get worn down and they", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.2080378742395933, "w": 0.8259900604107785, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "eventually fall out, so they continually grow new teeth that get pushed forward to", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.23636822431373095, "w": 0.8264039536492885, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "replace the ones they lose. Instead of having incisors to grasp their food,", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.26469857438786853, "w": 0.8267884222260566, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "manatees have lips which function like a pair of hands to help tear food away from", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.2930660423122369, "w": 0.8262079259376137, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "the seafloor.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.32139637352567824, "w": 0.13287859415322745, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "Manatees are fully aquatic, but as mammals, they need to come up to the surface", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.3497267990426009, "w": 0.82620782341266, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "to breathe. When awake, they typically surface every two to four minutes, but they", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.3780571491167385, "w": 0.8267215759561759, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "can hold their breath for much longer. Adult manatees sleep underwater for 10-12", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.40638753691226864, "w": 0.8268527053720923, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "hours a day, but they come up for air every 15-20 minutes. Active manatees need", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.4347178869864063, "w": 0.8265162184737048, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "to breathe more frequently. It's thought that manatees use their muscular", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.4630482747819364, "w": 0.8260771040965588, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "diaphragm and breathing to adjust their buoyancy. They may use diaphragm", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.49140337008956114, "w": 0.826107451482885, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "contractions to compress and store gas in folds in their large intestine to help", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5197337201636988, "w": 0.8258159730392179, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "them float.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5480641079592289, "w": 0.11594652113864624, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "The West Indian manatee reaches about 3.5 metres long and weighs on average", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5763944580333665, "w": 0.8259901629357324, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "around 500 kilogrammes. It moves between fresh water and salt water, taking", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6047248458288966, "w": 0.8261877285217142, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "advantage of coastal mangroves and coral reefs, rivers, lakes and inland lagoons.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6330551959030343, "w": 0.8260337360410994, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "There are two subspecies of West Indian manatee: The Antillean manatee is found", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6612743433120499, "w": 0.8263384402038073, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "in waters from the Bahamas to Brazil, whereas the Florida manatee is found in US", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6896046933861876, "w": 0.8265517946326751, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "waters, although some individuals have been recorded in the Bahamas. In winter,", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7179351189031102, "w": 0.8257940326991037, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "the Florida manatee is typically restricted to Florida. When the ambient water", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7462654689772478, "w": 0.826099044436673, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "temperature drops below 20°C, it takes refuge in naturally and artificially warmed", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7745958190513854, "w": 0.8266836417232681, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "water, such as at the warm-water outfalls from powerplants.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8029261691255231, "w": 0.6192052767953324, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "The African manatee is also about 3.5 metres long and found in the sea along the", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8312565191996607, "w": 0.8263384402038073, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "west coast of Africa, from Mauritania down to Angola. The species also makes use", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8596116899500704, "w": 0.8264255864145414, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "of rivers, with the mammals seen in landlocked countries such as Mali and Niger.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8879420400242081, "w": 0.8214158070718227, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}], "source": "pdf text layer"}